@inject Translator Translator

@{ UseReportCulture(); }

@* The stations where passengers change trains, under their own heading.

   Printed only where the author wrote an <Interchanges/> tag. Unlike the shunting yards, which also
   sit on the layout page at the back of the booklet, this list has no fixed home: it belongs to the
   meeting's own conventions about passenger tickets, and only the author knows which part of their
   text it explains.

   A plain list of names rather than a table, because there is only one thing to say about each
   station — that a passenger may change trains there. The stations come in layout order, so a reader
   following the line from one end to the other meets them in the order they will.

   No styling of its own: the heading and the paragraph take .instructions-body's own rules, which is
   also what InstructionsPagination.InterchangesHeight is derived from. *@

@if (Interchanges.Count > 0)
{
    <h2>@Translator("Interchanges")</h2>
    <p>@string.Join(", ", Interchanges.Select(station => station.Name))</p>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The layout whose passenger interchanges are listed. A layout not worked with passenger tickets
    /// has none, and prints nothing at all — the tag then costs the booklet nothing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Layout? Layout { get; set; }

    private IReadOnlyList<Station> Interchanges => Layout?.PassengerInterchanges ?? [];
}
